WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.781
Welcome to the
Ticket-to-Machine Reference,

2
00:00:02.781 --> 00:00:06.575
Appendix B of Book 13 in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:09.923
Chapter 10 makes an argument
about operational tempo;

4
00:00:09.923 --> 00:00:12.825
this appendix is the
reference that backs it up.

5
00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:15.708
Over the next twelve
minutes we'll walk the loop,

6
00:00:15.708 --> 00:00:20.662
the five clauses every change passes through,
the lanes each kind of change takes,

7
00:00:20.662 --> 00:00:24.137
the clocks, the evidence record,
and four worked examples.

8
00:00:24.137 --> 00:00:28.734
One caution up front: the lanes,
ceilings, severities and windows are the

9
00:00:28.734 --> 00:00:33.799
program's design, not federal requirements,
and the examples are illustrative.

10
00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:36.400
Let's start with why any of this matters.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.750
Here's the idea the
whole appendix rests on.

12
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.633
A ticket that sends a person to
a console doesn't prove anything;

13
00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:48.425
it just changes who's to blame.

14
00:00:48.425 --> 00:00:53.188
The person does the work, replies
"done," and the record holds a claim.

15
00:00:53.188 --> 00:00:57.188
Now flip it: the person approves,
a machine runs a versioned

16
00:00:57.188 --> 00:01:01.188
plan under its own identity,
and a re-read proves the result.

17
00:01:01.188 --> 00:01:05.375
The person keeps the one job a
machine can't do, which is to decide.

18
00:01:05.375 --> 00:01:08.576
And this matters because a
platform that finds drift

19
00:01:08.576 --> 00:01:12.213
perfectly but closes it slowly
is just documenting neglect.

20
00:01:12.213 --> 00:01:15.060
So what does the machine
path look like end to end?

21
00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.485
It's a loop of five steps.

22
00:01:20.485 --> 00:01:25.628
A rule tests actual state against the
record, a difference becomes a typed finding,

23
00:01:25.628 --> 00:01:29.788
the finding becomes a task with an
owner and a clock, the change runs

24
00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:33.872
on the platform's own surface, and
a re-read closes it with evidence.

25
00:01:33.872 --> 00:01:37.420
The important part is what the
coordination layer doesn't do:

26
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.336
it never makes the change itself.

27
00:01:39.336 --> 00:01:43.798
Its job is to make every drift
owned, clocked, approved and proven.

28
00:01:43.798 --> 00:01:47.504
Federal policy is heading the
same way for cloud providers,

29
00:01:47.504 --> 00:01:51.210
and the program applies that
logic to an agency's own estate.

30
00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.720
Inside that loop, every
request runs a chain.

31
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.224
Every governed change,
whether it's an add, a move,

32
00:02:02.224 --> 00:02:06.845
a deletion or a reset, passes
the same five clauses, in order.

33
00:02:06.845 --> 00:02:08.801
It has to trace to a real origin,

34
00:02:08.801 --> 00:02:13.810
carry an approval from someone who isn't
the requester, run under a short-lived

35
00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.270
scoped role, prove its result with a
re-read, and write its own evidence.

36
00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:23.358
If any clause fails, the chain stops,
and the task isn't treated as closed.

37
00:02:23.358 --> 00:02:27.645
And here's the highpoint: a refusal
is recorded just like a success,

38
00:02:27.645 --> 00:02:30.821
so a blocked self-approval
becomes evidence too.

39
00:02:30.821 --> 00:02:33.108
Next question: who approves what?

40
00:02:33.108 --> 00:02:34.980
That's where the lanes come in.

41
00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:43.677
Every kind of change takes one of five
lanes, and the lane settles three things:

42
00:02:43.677 --> 00:02:47.542
who approves, what actually runs,
and what evidence comes out.

43
00:02:47.542 --> 00:02:49.418
Two lanes run on machines.

44
00:02:49.418 --> 00:02:54.431
The gated lane, where a named person
approves each instance, is the default.

45
00:02:54.431 --> 00:02:56.923
The autonomous lane is only for narrow,

46
00:02:56.923 --> 00:03:02.143
low-risk classes the authorizing official
has accepted in writing, ahead of time.

47
00:03:02.143 --> 00:03:06.305
Then there's a human-decision lane, a
protocol lane with no ticket at all,

48
00:03:06.305 --> 00:03:09.679
and the declared exception
for when no machine can reach.

49
00:03:09.679 --> 00:03:12.267
Remember, this is the program's design.

50
00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:14.260
So which changes land where?

51
00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:23.099
Before any class goes to a machine, it
has to pass six tests from Chapter 10:

52
00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:26.407
reachable, deterministic,
bounded, reversible,

53
00:03:26.407 --> 00:03:29.885
verifiable, and not on the
permanently-gated list.

54
00:03:29.885 --> 00:03:34.010
With that filter in place, the starting
assignment falls out naturally.

55
00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.672
Routine configuration drift and patching
run on machines behind an approval.

56
00:03:38.672 --> 00:03:43.355
Anything critical, or anything
touching authorization or provenance,

57
00:03:43.355 --> 00:03:45.022
halts and goes to a person.

58
00:03:45.022 --> 00:03:47.599
Access requests ride the five clauses,

59
00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:51.384
and an idle entitlement goes
to its owner to decide first.

60
00:03:51.384 --> 00:03:54.435
An agency tunes all of
this in its own record.

61
00:03:54.435 --> 00:03:56.350
Here's the rest of the table.

62
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.412
The second half covers the
changes that need the most care.

63
00:04:03.412 --> 00:04:08.118
Anything with a big blast radius, like
a tenant-wide role or a Conditional

64
00:04:08.118 --> 00:04:12.824
Access change, stays gated permanently,
however mature the program gets.

65
00:04:12.824 --> 00:04:17.312
Changes to the record itself are always
a human decision through review.

66
00:04:17.312 --> 00:04:19.796
Routine certificates run on protocol,

67
00:04:19.796 --> 00:04:22.900
and emergencies act first
and record afterward.

68
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:24.825
Two rules hold across all of it.

69
00:04:24.825 --> 00:04:28.661
A machine never edits the
record to make drift disappear,

70
00:04:28.661 --> 00:04:31.763
and no gap quietly goes
to a person at a console.

71
00:04:31.763 --> 00:04:35.270
Now let's look at drift from
the other side, by class.

72
00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.495
Drift comes in five classes,
and each has a default lane.

73
00:04:42.495 --> 00:04:47.120
A schema problem, where the format's
wrong, can often be fixed by a machine.

74
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.452
Provenance and authorization
drift are different:

75
00:04:50.452 --> 00:04:54.558
when the claim is in active use,
the run halts and a person decides.

76
00:04:54.558 --> 00:04:58.558
Semantic and identity drift
usually need human judgment too.

77
00:04:58.558 --> 00:05:02.439
The honest limit here is that
the classes whose fixes depend on

78
00:05:02.439 --> 00:05:06.544
context reach a person more often,
and that's by design, not a failure.

79
00:05:06.544 --> 00:05:11.090
Which brings us to the clock: how fast
does each finding have to be handled?

80
00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.690
The tempo puts a clock on every severity.

81
00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:20.376
Critical findings never get a machine fix;

82
00:05:20.376 --> 00:05:23.752
the run halts and a named
person decides immediately.

83
00:05:23.752 --> 00:05:27.778
High findings get an hour, medium
a day, and low three days.

84
00:05:27.778 --> 00:05:31.353
These windows bound the
response, not the drift itself;

85
00:05:31.353 --> 00:05:35.166
a program with no findings is
more likely blind than clean.

86
00:05:35.166 --> 00:05:36.540
Two things to remember.

87
00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.577
Automatic fixes hold only for
classes accepted in advance.

88
00:05:40.577 --> 00:05:44.646
And when an outside clock is
shorter, like CISA's vulnerability

89
00:05:44.646 --> 00:05:48.791
timelines or the one-hour incident
notice, the outside clock wins.

90
00:05:48.791 --> 00:05:51.190
So how does leadership know it's working?

91
00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:58.977
These five measures belong to
leadership, not just operations.

92
00:05:58.977 --> 00:06:01.303
How much of the estate can we even see?

93
00:06:01.303 --> 00:06:03.752
Do findings close inside their windows?

94
00:06:03.752 --> 00:06:05.941
How often does a person have to step in?

95
00:06:05.941 --> 00:06:08.152
What share of closures did a machine do?

96
00:06:08.152 --> 00:06:10.739
And how many tasks still
have no machine at all?

97
00:06:10.739 --> 00:06:13.453
Each bad value tells
you something specific,

98
00:06:13.453 --> 00:06:16.240
like manual work quietly
becoming permanent.

99
00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.653
The good news is that none of this
needs round-the-clock staffing.

100
00:06:19.653 --> 00:06:22.935
What it does need is named
people in the escalation roles,

101
00:06:22.935 --> 00:06:25.240
because tooling can't create authority.

102
00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.830
Now, what does all that work leave behind?

103
00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.118
It leaves one record, in one
shape, whatever the lane.

104
00:06:35.118 --> 00:06:40.491
A machine fix, a recorded human session
and an automated certificate renewal all

105
00:06:40.491 --> 00:06:44.902
fill the same fields: where it came
from, who owned it, who approved it,

106
00:06:44.902 --> 00:06:48.592
what identity ran it, what ran,
and what the re-read showed.

107
00:06:48.592 --> 00:06:49.630
That's the point.

108
00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:52.720
Nobody assembles evidence
by hand at audit time,

109
00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.742
and an assessor reads one format whatever
the platform, and checks four things:

110
00:06:57.742 --> 00:07:00.292
a prior approval, a controlled artifact,

111
00:07:00.292 --> 00:07:03.305
a logged execution and a
result they can re-test.

112
00:07:03.305 --> 00:07:06.367
Even the human session lane
fills the same fields,

113
00:07:06.367 --> 00:07:09.280
just with a recording
where the run log would be.

114
00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.750
To make this concrete, let's
walk through some examples.

115
00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:20.275
Here are the first two examples,
both invented to show the pattern.

116
00:07:20.275 --> 00:07:24.325
In the first, a sharing setting
drifts to allow anonymous links.

117
00:07:24.325 --> 00:07:28.859
The authorizing official has accepted
that one narrow fix in advance,

118
00:07:28.859 --> 00:07:31.282
so the machine restores it, re-reads it,

119
00:07:31.282 --> 00:07:35.737
and closes the task with one record,
with no one waiting on an approval.

120
00:07:35.737 --> 00:07:40.400
If it drifts again, that's a question
for the steward, not another silent fix.

121
00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.199
In the second, an access
request walks all five clauses,

122
00:07:44.199 --> 00:07:47.575
and the role is granted as
eligible, not standing.

123
00:07:47.575 --> 00:07:51.790
What happens when a machine
shouldn't, or can't, make the fix?

124
00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:57.540
These two show the harder paths.

125
00:07:57.540 --> 00:07:58.797
In the third example,

126
00:07:58.797 --> 00:08:02.865
the inventory finds a certificate
from an issuer nobody approved.

127
00:08:02.865 --> 00:08:04.255
There's no automatic fix,

128
00:08:04.255 --> 00:08:07.940
because replacing it blindly
could break whoever depends on it.

129
00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.850
The PKI owner decides to replace
it through the approved issuer

130
00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:15.465
and block the rogue one, and then
the gated lane does the work.

131
00:08:15.465 --> 00:08:20.228
In the fourth, an old appliance has only
a console, so no machine can reach it.

132
00:08:20.228 --> 00:08:21.938
The owner approves a short,

133
00:08:21.938 --> 00:08:25.903
recorded session with a compensating
control and an end date.

134
00:08:25.903 --> 00:08:28.841
It's visible, approved
first and temporary.

135
00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:31.960
Certificates deserve a
closer look of their own.

136
00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:39.798
Certificates are the deliberate
exception, and the reason is volume.

137
00:08:39.798 --> 00:08:44.453
Public TLS lifetimes are shrinking
from three hundred ninety-eight

138
00:08:44.453 --> 00:08:48.373
days to forty-seven by 2029,
so renewals come continuously.

139
00:08:48.373 --> 00:08:51.332
Putting each one through an
approval wouldn't add rigor;

140
00:08:51.332 --> 00:08:54.485
it would just teach approvers
to click yes without reading.

141
00:08:54.485 --> 00:08:58.716
So routine renewal runs on
protocol and renewal engines,

142
00:08:58.716 --> 00:09:01.147
with policy partly living in DNS.

143
00:09:01.147 --> 00:09:04.858
The anomalies still come through
the front door, like a new issuing

144
00:09:04.858 --> 00:09:08.973
authority or an unknown issuer, because
they're rare and they need a person.

145
00:09:08.973 --> 00:09:12.340
Speaking of the front door,
there should only be one.

146
00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:19.359
Lots of tools detect
problems: posture engines,

147
00:09:19.359 --> 00:09:23.215
scanners, entitlement tools,
certificate inventories.

148
00:09:23.215 --> 00:09:24.540
The rule is simple.

149
00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.648
Each one is a detection engine
feeding the one coordination layer,

150
00:09:28.648 --> 00:09:30.515
never a second queue of its own.

151
00:09:30.515 --> 00:09:35.644
That way an over-privileged role and an open
storage account land in the same place,

152
00:09:35.644 --> 00:09:37.378
under the same five clauses,

153
00:09:37.378 --> 00:09:41.753
and access requests from a governance
front end close the same way too.

154
00:09:41.753 --> 00:09:46.257
Two cautions keep this honest: check
each tool's real platform coverage,

155
00:09:46.257 --> 00:09:49.846
since on-premises virtualization
kept coming up as a gap,

156
00:09:49.846 --> 00:09:52.977
and give the alternatives
a fair build-or-buy look.

157
00:09:52.977 --> 00:09:55.080
So where does this pattern break?

158
00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.863
This slide is worth slowing down on,

159
00:10:00.863 --> 00:10:04.718
because every row is a way the
pattern fails while looking correct.

160
00:10:04.718 --> 00:10:09.448
A security review of a reference build
found origins that weren't checked,

161
00:10:09.448 --> 00:10:14.101
commands built from unescaped strings,
input that could swap the target,

162
00:10:14.101 --> 00:10:16.738
a password crossing a queue in plain text,

163
00:10:16.738 --> 00:10:21.468
a leg that treated "sent" as "done," and
tests that never touched the risky path.

164
00:10:21.468 --> 00:10:23.418
Each fix became a design rule.

165
00:10:23.418 --> 00:10:27.566
The lesson is humbling: tests
prove only the refusals they test,

166
00:10:27.566 --> 00:10:30.917
and a reference build is a
starting point, not proof.

167
00:10:30.917 --> 00:10:32.900
So, what's left to decide?

168
00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:40.150
The reference leaves a short
list of decisions to the agency.

169
00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:41.947
Which narrow classes, if any,

170
00:10:41.947 --> 00:10:45.775
the authorizing official
accepts for autonomous closure.

171
00:10:45.775 --> 00:10:48.112
Who actually holds the escalation roles.

172
00:10:48.112 --> 00:10:51.037
How classes map to lanes
in your own record.

173
00:10:51.037 --> 00:10:53.588
What targets you set
for the five measures.

174
00:10:53.588 --> 00:10:56.238
And an end date for
every declared exception.

175
00:10:56.238 --> 00:11:00.043
For the full argument, read
Chapter 10, then the drift engine,

176
00:11:00.043 --> 00:11:03.849
the evidence chapter and the
control mapping in Appendix A.

177
00:11:03.849 --> 00:11:04.950
Thanks for watching.

178
00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.746
Remember: a ticket is
evidence only when it

179
00:11:07.746 --> 00:11:11.330
authorized something that
ran, and a re-read proved it.
